	affiliations = {Electrical and Electronics Engineering Department, KIT-KalaignarKarunanidhi Institute of Technology, Tamil Nadu, Coimbatore, 641402, India; SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Automobile Engineering, Chennai, 603203, India; Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Hyderabad, 501218, India; Chennai Institute of Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Chennai, 600069, India; Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia, Electronic and Systems Engineering, Department of Electrical, Faculty Engineering and Built Environment, Selangor, Bangi, 43600, Malaysia},
	abstract = {Switched reluctance motors (SRMs) offer robust and cost-effective solutions for various applications due to their simple structure, high efficiency, and fault tolerance. Effective torque and flux control in SRMs provides benefits such as high torque density, wide speed range operation, and improved efficiency. However, challenges remain in mitigating torque ripple, achieving precise flux regulation, and handling nonlinear magnetic characteristics. Direct torque control (DTC) approach SRM for simple control implementation and improved steady-state torque performance compared to conventional control schemes. DTC offers high torque and flux control accuracy, robustness against parameter variations and disturbances, and provides a low dynamic response. However, DTC techniques can result in high torque ripple due to using two hysteresis torques and flux bands within the limited loop, improper selection of sectors, and voltage vector (VV) switching. To address this issue, this article proposes a DTC approach based on a 16 VV selection, eight sectors using an adaptive neuro-fuzzy inference system (ANFIS) torque band. In addition, two switching instant regulators (SIRs) are introduced to further enhance performance. These regulators not only keep the torque within the hysteresis bounds but also minimize torque ripple without increasing the average switching frequency. The proposed solutions are compared to conventional DTC schemes, and experimental evaluations demonstrate significant reductions in torque ripple, flux ripple, and current distortion. © 2013 IEEE.},
