	abstract = {Vehicle-to-Vehicle (V2V) charging has become one of the strategies to be considered as an efficient energy sharing of the electric vehicles (EVs) to ensure the reduction of range anxiety and the lack of strong dependence on the fixed charging stations. Nevertheless, the current V2V charging models are prone to several power conversion steps, higher power losses and large exterior interfaces. Traditional methods of V2V charging that are based on off-board units of power transfer tend to make systems more costly and bulky, and the on-board type-2 chargers are inefficient because of the multi/repetitive phases of power conversion. In order to address these constraints, this paper introduces a new V2V energy transfer method where direct battery-to-battery connection has been proposed, using type-2 AC charging input ports by making use of controlled switching arrangements. The proposed technique will have better power transfer efficiency by removing multiple conversion processes. The on-board converter is based on Insulated Gate Bipolar Transistors (IGBTs) to provide an efficient switching, less power waste, and additional energy flow control between vehicles. Moreover, there is a fuzzy logic control scheme that is used to dynamically control power transfer depending on the battery conditions of the involved EVs to achieve a fair and optimal energy transfer. The proposed system is analyzed on the basis of the MATLAB/Simulink simulations and evaluated in different V2V charging conditions, the feasibility of which is tested on the basis of a scaled experimental prototype. The findings indicate that IGBT based converters, optimization of power transfer topology and smart control are beneficial in enhancing V2V charging as these combined methods are promising and sustainable in future electric mobility implementation.  © 2026 IEEE.},
