	title = {Enhancing Retail Efficiency with a Smart Trolley: RFID-Based Billing and Bluetooth Integration},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {2025 IEEE 4th World Conference on Applied Intelligence and Computing, AIC 2025},
	pages = {455 - 460},
	doi = {10.1109/AIC66080.2025.11211875},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023635948?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {St. Martin's Engineering College, Department of EEE, Telangana, Secunderabad, 500100, India; St. Martin's Engineering College, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Telangana, Secunderabad, 500100, India; Vardhaman College of Engineering, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Telangana, Shamshabad, India; College of Engineering and Technology, University of Technology and Applied Sciences, Engineering Department, Ibra, Oman; Anurag University, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, 500088, India},
	abstract = {The number of people of all ages who are interested in electronic gadgets has increased significantly because of the rapid advancement of technology. Increasingly, electronic gadgets such as barcode scanners, RFID scanners, and smart card readers are being utilized in a variety of different economic sectors. The same kinds of devices are required by supermarkets as well. Now, each and every customer in the shopping centre purchases the item that is placed in the cart. After making a purchase, the individual will be required to wait in line for the charging process. Every product's barcode is scanned by an employee throughout the billing process, and the consumer is then billed for the product. The time it takes to complete this process can be quite lengthy, and it may be much more time-consuming on weekends, holidays, or special deals. A clever method of shopping in shopping malls has been developed as a solution to this problem. There is an RFID tag attached to each product rather than a barcode. An RFID reader, an LCD display, and a Bluetooth module are all included in the Smart Trolley. Within this system is an ultrasonic sensor that serves as an obstacle alert system. Additionally, we are able to control the robot through the use of a Bluetooth application. Every time a customer sets a product on the trolley, that product is scanned, and the information that is shown includes the product's name, price, and expiration date. At the end of the checkout process, the total cost will be added to the bill. Along with being recorded in the memory of the microcontroller, the bill is also transferred through the Bluetooth module in order to be checked by the counter using the Android application. Following the completion of the transaction, the consumer receives a notification via the Bluetooth module that contains the specifics of the purchase. For the purpose of programming, the Arduino IDE software tool is utilized, and the Proteus software is utilized to verify the outcomes of simulations prior to the implementation of hardware.  © 2025 IEEE.},
