	abstract = {The continuous miniaturization of semiconductor technology and the emergence of quantum-enhanced architectures have accelerated the development of energy-efficient quantum systems. Sequential circuits based on conventional CMOS technologies face difficulties with increasing logic density, as they are subject to information loss, excessive heat dissipation, higher gate delays, and limited fault detection capability. Due to these challenges, CMOS logic-based integrated systems are not scalable, reliable, or energy-efficient, which has led to a shift toward quantum-compatible designs. In this research, two designs are proposed to overcome these challenges, namely reversible D flip-flops (D-FFs) with testable features that integrate quantum-equivalent logic, and dual-edge triggered master-slave mechanisms that enhance switching performance. In a dual-edge triggered master-slave configuration, IBM Qiskit is utilized to validate the design. We synthesized a quantum assembly code (QAC) to test qubit behavior under realistic noise conditions in the dual-edge triggered D-FF design. The dual-edge triggered D-FF architecture requires 6 gates, with a quantum cost of 29 and a circuit depth of 20. The results show that the proposed design is suitable for energy-efficient quantum computing systems. In summary, the results demonstrate that the quantum-equivalent reversible architecture improves computational performance as well as reliability while maintaining design-for-test (DFT) capabilities. This proposed testable D-FF bridges the gap between Design for Testability (DFT) and quantum computing implementation by delivering scalable, low-power, and fault-tolerant sequential elements. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
