	abstract = {In this work, suitable control methods are tested on the interconnected power system (IPS) network that could ensure the operation of the electrical system is reliable and stable. The investigations had been performed in the simulation platform by designing the multi-area contemporary power system (MACPS) that comprises geothermal along with hydrothermal units. The analysis is begun by laying a 10 % step load disturbance in area 1 under the supervised control action of the tilt-integral-derivative plus filter (TIDF) regulator. The optimization approach of the fruit fly algorithm (FFA) is enforced for the optimal functioning of the regulator. Moreover, the efficacy of the TIDF regulator in handling the uncertainties and the dynamic behavior of the MACPS is illustrated using other control frameworks reported in literature. However, the impact of uncertainty constraints on the MACPS performance is revealed and justified in the result section. Further, the analysis is extended on the MACPS by incorporating a unified power flow controller (UPFC) with a tie-line and the control regions with superconducting magnetic energy storage (SMES) devices for proving additional control strategies. The simulation results confirmed the enrichment in MACPS dynamic behavior with the adaptation of an additional control approach. © 2025 IEEE.},
