	affiliations = {Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of Eee, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The growing world requirement for renewable energy has accelerated the effective integration of renewable energy sources (RES) like solar, wind, and fuel cells into contemporary power systems. But the variable and intermittent nature of these sources is quite challenging for the aim of a stable and secure energy supply. In response to this, the application of Multi-Port Converters (MPCS) has come forward as a hopeful solution. This project involves the design, modelling, and implementation of an optimal Multi-Port Converter system for integrating multiple renewable energy sources into a common load or grid without any intervening discontinuity. The suggested system architec- ture consists of a multi-input single-output (MISO) DC-DC converter topology capable of simultaneously interfacing disparate RES with dissimilar voltage and power ratings. Such a topology brings with it many benefits like component minimisation, small size, low cost, and better power management. There are intelligent algorithms for power flow control implemented within the system, which dynamically give priority to sources of energy based on their availabil- ity, efficiency, and current demand. Moreover, a storage device is included to compensate for the generationload mismatch and provide continuity of power and enhance the reliability of the overall system. The converter is tested for operation under diverse environmental and load conditions, and MAT- LAB/Simulink simulation tools are utilised to evaluate the performance of the system. The key performance factors, like efficiency of conversion, regulation of voltage, utilisation of the source, and stability of the system, are measured and optimised. The project also deals with real-world issues like Maximum Power Point Tracking (MPPT) for solar and wind, protection schemes, and thermal management. The result proves that MPCS are capable of efficiently managing the integration of hybrid renewable systems without compromising system efficiency and power quality. The method not only increases the utili- sation of green energy but also develops sustainable and smart energy systems appropriate for standalone and grid applications © 2025 IEEE.},
