	abstract = {Calcium concentrations in water treatment plants must be carefully monitored to avoid fouling, scaling, and membrane performance degradation. A novel approach to controlling calcium concentrations in hybrid reverse osmosis/membrane distillation systems in real time using fuzzy-enhanced deep reinforcement learning (FD-RL) has been developed. By combining fuzzy logic with the deep reinforcement learning (DRL) agent's ability to learn about the optimal operation of a simulated hybrid system environment, this new approach allows for the rapid adaptation of the DRL agent to changing conditions in the water chemistry. By dynamically optimizing several critical input parameters (e.g., pH, antiscalant dosage rate, and operating pressure), the FD-RL model improves desalination efficiency by minimizing the precipitation of calcium while also enhancing the overall performance of the hybrid RO-MD system. The FD-RL model has been validated through experiments with synthetic water profiles and demonstrated to provide over 95 % accuracy in predicting calcium concentrations and outperforming conventional model-based and rule-based systems for the management of calcium concentrations in seawater. Using the fuzzy inference system facilitates seamless decision-making during periods of variable water quality. In addition, the deep learning module allows for long-term adaptation to continued changes in operation of the hybrid RO–MD system. This method represents a significant advancement in the development of water quality enhancement and, ultimately, the consistent and sustainable operation of hybrid RO–MD systems. © 2025},
