	abstract = {The application of Brushless DC (BLDC) motors in electric vehicles, robotics and industrial automation is all-encompassing, and it is because this type of motor is highly efficient, reliable, and has very low maintenance needs. Nevertheless, to effectively control the BLDC motors in real-time requires precision in commutation and a rapid speed control in the dynamic working conditions. The article examines how a control system using FPGA hardware can be developed to operate BLDC motors by leveraging the parallel processing nature of FPGA hardware. The algorithms of motor control, such as pulse width modulation (PWM), are carried out and tested on a FPGA platform. Response time and torque ripple are the main performance parameters that are experimentally evaluated and compared to traditional microcontroller-based control schemes. These findings indicate FPGA-based control is quicker in dynamic response, decreased torque ripple, and increased motor efficiency in total, which is why it would be appropriate in high-speed, real-time motor control.  © 2026 IEEE.},
