	affiliations = {Department of EEE, Hyderabad Institute of Technology and Management, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; Department of EEE, Vardhaman College of Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) continues to surge globally, and the demand for fast charging infrastructure has become a critical concern. Fast charging technology plays a pivotal role in reducing range anxiety and enhancing the practicality of EVs. It aims to provide an in-depth analysis of the current issues and future challenges associated with the fast charging of EVs and its impact on the power quality of the grid. It gives the state of fast charging technology for EVs, highlighting the various charging standards, connector types, and power levels used across different regions. It also discusses the key players in the fast charging infrastructure market, emphasizing the need for interoperability and standardization to ensure the seamless integration of EVs into the existing power grid. Rapid and high-power charging can lead to voltage and current fluctuations, harmonics, and power factor issues. It explores the technical challenges in maintaining grid stability and power quality in the presence of fast chargers and outlines potential solutions such as advanced power electronics, grid management strategies, and improved communication protocols between EVs and the grid. It discusses the energy sources powering fast chargers, including the integration of renewable energy, and their environmental implications. Additionally, the economic aspects of fast charging infrastructure deployment and the cost-benefit analysis of grid upgrades are examined, taking into account the increasing demand for electricity and the potential need for grid expansion. It presents an outlook on the future challenges and opportunities in the context of fast charging and grid power quality. This includes advancements in battery technology, smart grid solutions, and the potential for bidirectional power flow between EVs and the grid. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Battery electric vehicles (BEVs); control engineering; energy application; energy management; energy storage; energy sustainability; EVs; fast charging; grid; plug-in hybrid electric vehicles},
