	title = {Pelican Optimization Algorithm based Maximum Power Point Tracking of PV System under Partial Shading},
	year = {2026},
	journal = {2026 International Conference on Electric Power and Renewable Energy, EPREC 2026},
	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/EPREC66546.2026.11411949},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036373267?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Sona College of Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Salem, 05, India; Chaitanya Bharathi Institute of Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; Aarupadai Veedu Institute of Technology, VMRF(DU), Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Paiyanoor, India; Sona College of Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Tamil Nadu, Salem, India; Graphic Era Deemed to Be University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Dehradun, India; New Prince Shri Bhavani College of Engineering and Technology, Department of CSE, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, India; Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of EEE, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; New Horizon College of Engineering, Department of ECE, Karnataka, Bangalore, India; CPGC Pvt. Ltd, ERG Foundation, Andhra Pradesh, Visakhapatnam, India},
	abstract = {Electricity is necessary in all locations for numerous purposes in order to accomplish a variety of tasks. The utilization of solar energy for generating electricity presents a viable solution to numerous challenges. Solar panels are commonly utilized to generate electrical power from sunlight. It is essential to integrate a MPPTC into the PVS in order to optimize its efficiency and ensure maximum utilization. The paper chooses a DC to DC forward circuit to function as MPPTC. Several traditional algorithms, such as incremental conductance and P&O, can be utilized successfully for MPPTC in consistent solar irradiance conditions. Nevertheless, PVMs connected to form a power supply unit may experience varying irradiances caused by factors such as trees, shading, dust, birds, clouds, and other environmental elements. This occurrence leads the system to experience PSC. Conventional methods are not able to catch the optimal location for maximizing energy harvest under PSC. Hence, it is needed to implement an optimization method to maximize the efficiency of the PVS during PSC operation. The paper introduces a new optimization method called the "Pelican Optimization Algorithm (POA)"for MPPTC to increase energy harvesting during PSCs. This paper also encompasses a comparison of POA, with existing methods such are WOA, BOA, HOA, and MIWO. The HIL system is implemented through the utilization of OPAL-RT technology in order to gather diverse data for conducting analysis across varying operational scenarios. © 2026 IEEE.},
