	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85205184958?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, St. Martin’s Engineering College Secunderabad, Telangana, India; Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Koneru Lakshmaiah Education Foundation, Andhra Pradesh, Guntur, India; Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Anurag University, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Vardhaman College of Engineering, Kacharam, Shamshabad, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Vasireddy Venkatadri Institute of Technology, Andhra Pradesh, Guntur, India; Department of Nuclear and Renewable Energy, Ural Federal University Named After the First President of Russia Boris Yeltsin, Ekaterinburg, Russian Federation; Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Vardhaman College of Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {Electric vehicles (EVs) play a significant role in the administration of the energy storage for the microgrid in an efficient manner. This process is managed by equipment that connects the grid to vehicles, which can store energy, and technology that connects vehicles to the grid, which can return the energy that was stored in the vehicle to the grid. In order to transform this idea into a product that actually does something, we are going to need an acceptable architecture. The architecture that is presented in this work takes advantage of DC quick charging in order to develop a vehicle-to-grid (V2G) and grid-to-vehicle (G2V) idea. This was done in order to produce a V2G and G2V concept. Microgrids have the capability to use the batteries that are present in EVs as potential energy storage systems. EVs are also known as hybrid EVs (HEVs). They are able to help with the management of the energy that is present in the microgrid by storing energy in the case that there is an excess (a process known as G2V) and then resupplying energy to the grid (V2G). The necessary control systems as well as technological breakthroughs need to be established in order to put this idea into practice. An architecture for quick charging of level-3 EVs in a V2G-G2V system that runs on a microgrid is proposed in this work. The system operates on a microgrid. One of the components of the modeled microgrid test system for the electric car interface is a DC rapid charging station. This component is part of the model. The flow of power from a generator to a user is demonstrated in this computer-based simulation of the process. The results of the studies suggest that the operating modes G2V-V2G are utilized by the batteries found in EVs in order to actively regulate the amount of electricity supplied by the microgrid. The design of the charging station ensures that grid-injected current has minimal harmonic distortion, and that the controller has strong dynamic abilities of maintaining DC bus voltage stability. Additionally, the architecture of the charging station is responsible for its construction. In addition to this, the design of the charging station ensures that the current that is fed into the grid has a lower level of harmonic distortion. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {charging; electric vehicle; fast charging; grid connected inverter; Grid to vehicle; inverter; inverter control; microgrid; off-board charger; vehicle to grid; voltage},
