	abstract = {The purpose of this study is to provide a continuous flow of AC power, at a voltage of 230 volts, to a single-phase load. This is accomplished by automatically transferring the load from the missing phase to the next available phase in a three-phase system. It has been observed that 70% of power outages in distribution systems are caused by single-phase failures, while the other two phases remain operational. As a result, in any commercial or domestic power system that has three phases, it's advisable to have an automatic switching system in place to ensure uninterrupted power to critical loads in case of a missing phase. The automatic selection in this three-phase supply system is accomplished through the use of interconnecting relays, with one relay feeding the load at all times. In the event of a phase failure, the corresponding step-down transformer is activated through a transistor assembly. The three phases are referred to as the R phase, Y phase, and B phase. To manage the switching of the phases, there are three switches. The LED on the left indicates which phase is on or off, while the LED and relays on the right show which supply is powering the load.  © 2023 IEEE.},
