	affiliations = {School of Sciences and Humanities, SR University, Department of Physics, Telangana, Warangal, India; G. Pullaiah College of Engineering and Technology, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, A. P, Kurnool, India; Aditya University, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Andhra Pradesh, India; Kongu Engineering College, Department of EEE, Tamil Nadu, Erode, India; Graphic Era Deemed to Be University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Dehradun, India; New Prince Shri Bhavani College of Engineering and Technology, Department of EEE, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, India; Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of EEE, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; CPGC Pvt. Ltd and ERG Foundation, Andhra Pradesh, Visakhapatnam, India},
	abstract = {Among various electric motor drives, BLDC motor drives are gaining attention for numerous applications because of their unique features and characteristics. To operate the BLDC motor, the most commonly employed method is direct torque control (DTC). Traditional DTC models struggle to function efficiently when there are alterations in the source and swift fluctuations in load torque. Traditional PI controllers are unable to deliver precise output immediately during abrupt changes in the drive. Consequently, this paper proposes modified DTC utilizing super twisting sliding mode (STSM) controllers to operate the BLDC motor across different operating conditions. By integrating the regulation of the dc-link current (which serves as the input to the inverter) into the proposed control model, valuable speed and torque responses can be attained even amidst fluctuations in the power supply system. The proposed methodology is evaluated against several existing methodologies. Additionally, the design of the STSM controller has been integrated, and the PSO methodology has been developed to update the parameters of the STSM controllers. The suggested methodology is executed through the use of OPAL-RT units to demonstrate various outcomes of the hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) process. © 2025 IEEE.},
