	affiliations = {Chaitanya Bharathi Institute of Technology, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Hyderabad, India; Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia, Faculty of Engineering and Built Environment, Malaysia},
	abstract = {Electric Vehicles (EVs) are increasingly vital for sustainable transportation, yet the challenge of efficient charge scheduling persists due to the limited charging infrastructure and the growing number of EVs. This study introduces a charge scheduling algorithm based on War Strategy Optimization (WSO), drawing inspiration from military tactics like attack, defense, and troop reassignment, to reduce travel time, waiting time, and charging expenses. The algorithm dynamically allocates EVs to charging stations by considering real-time factors such as battery capacity, station capacity, wait times, and current traffic conditions. The effectiveness of the proposed WSO method is tested in a simulated setting with six origin/destination points and twelve charging stations, varying the number of EVs (25-100) and charging stations, and comparing its performance to Chaotic Harris Hawk Optimization (CHHO). The experimental results reveal that WSO significantly enhances scheduling efficiency, achieving a 39.67% decrease in waiting time, a 19.35% reduction in travel time, and a 8.36% decrease in charging costs compared to CHHO. These results underscore WSO's effectiveness in practical EV charge scheduling scenarios, promoting better resource use and lower operational costs. © 2025 IEEE.},
