	abstract = {Fuel cell-based electric vehicles (FCEVs) are gaining prominence as an attractive substitute for conventional internal combustion engines. They provide a zero-emission transportation option and address the limitations associated with battery EVs (BEVs). This chapter underscores the necessity of comprehensive models for FCEVs, encompassing fuel cell systems, powertrains, and overall vehicle dynamics, leveraging insights from the existing literature. These models facilitate simulation and optimization, empowering researchers and engineers to enhance FCEV efficiency and cost-effectiveness. Moreover, they play a pivotal role in evaluating FCEVs' impact on energy infrastructure and emissions reduction, guiding policymakers and urban planners. Examining fundamental principles, the review navigates FCEV evolution across diverse vehicle platforms, evaluating various fuel cell types. The evaluation of advancements in hydrogen infrastructure, refueling, and production methods is crucial for the widespread adoption of FCEVs. Environmental benefits, including greenhouse gas reduction, are elucidated. Challenges, such as cost reduction and competition with BEVs, are critically analyzed, offering a distinct perspective. The chapter concludes by emphasizing sustained research and investment to expedite FCEV integration into the global transportation landscape, recognizing its promise in ushering in a cleaner and more sustainable era. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
