	abstract = {The convergence of Artificial Intelligence (AI), Internet of Things (IoT), and Blockchain technologies is reshaping residential energy management through predictive optimization, decentralized governance, and secure peer-to-peer (P2P) transactions. This review synthesizes many studies using an enhanced Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA)-guided methodology that incorporates structured quality appraisal, deployment classification, and evidence synthesis. Unlike prior technology-focused surveys, the study integrates technical performance, regulatory preparedness, and socioeconomic feasibility within a unified socio-technical framework. Evidence from validated experiments and pilot deployments indicates that AI-enabled demand response and predictive control can reduce household energy consumption under context-specific conditions. At the same time, blockchain-based settlement improves transparency but introduces measurable computational overhead. To evaluate sustainability trade-offs, the review benchmarks consensus mechanisms and compares permissioned and public ledgers with respect to energy footprint, latency, and residential suitability. It further examines real-world deployments, technology readiness levels, explainable and privacy-preserving AI approaches, and emerging business models such as Energy-as-a-Service and community microgrids. These insights support progress toward SDG 7, SDG 11, and SDG 13 by enabling efficient, transparent, and low-carbon residential energy systems, while highlighting the need for lightweight architectures, federated intelligence, and adaptive regulatory frameworks for scalable implementation. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
