	abstract = {The World Sustainability Code emphasizes effective energy generation and its efficient utilization. This article focuses on optimizing energy generation and utilization to meet the load demand of an institutional building. The primary objective of this case study is to model and analyze the sustainability performance and reliability of a multi-renewable integrated system towards achieving net-zero energy building standards. The electrical load of a typical institutional building has been considered, and its energy requirements are met using renewable sources available on-site. Confidence in renewables and their effectiveness has been enhanced through extended hybrid modeling. The overall system performance and building energy conservation proficiency have been analyzed using simulated annealing optimization procedures. The building’s net-zero energy target will be demonstrated through optimal tri-objective measures, including technical, economic, and environmental performances, as well as the adoption of green hydrogen production from the institutional building. Various electrolysis-based hydrogen production methods have been analyzed considering the uncertain behavior of renewable inputs, predicting a better configuration to meet the building’s load demand with improved performance. A combination of 100 kW solar PV, 32 vertical axis building-mounted wind turbines, and a 10 kW Photon Exchange Membrane (PEM) electrolyzer with a 30 kW hydrogen fuel cell stack is proposed to meet the 72 kW daily load profile of the institutional building located in the southern region of India, ensuring smooth operation and cost-effectiveness towards achieving net-zero energy balance. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature B.V. 2024.},
