	pages = {187 - 191},
	doi = {10.1109/ICOECA68095.2026.11485501},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038100244?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of EEE, Telangana, Hyderabad, India},
	abstract = {The wind energy systems have constant changes in speed of wind particularly when it is not in the rated region. In these cases, there is a need to have a pitch control mechanism that helps control the output power, and also avoid unnecessary mechanical loading of generator and turbine parts. The traditional controllers like PID-based controllers tend to respond slowly to sudden wind gusts and the controllers may cause some observable power overshoot and torque shocks. This paper presents an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) as one of the intelligent pitch controllers to enhance high-wind operation stability of a small wind energy system. All the model is built and simulated using MATLAB/Simulink. Training data is first obtained with the help of a conventional pitch controller and a feedforward ANN is then trained on the Levenberg Marquardt algorithm to compute the optimum pitch angle as a reaction to changes in wind speed. The trained ANN controller is then incorporated into the system and tested on step wind. The results of the simulation affirm that the ANN controller holds the power to the rated level, improves dynamic response and the torque variations are considerably smaller in comparison to the traditional approach to control. The offered approach proves that the ANN-based pitch control can ensure safer and more consistent work in the unpredictable high-wind situations. © 2026 IEEE.},
