	abstract = {Building upon the Z-network perception and traditional isolated power converters. This manuscript presents a multi-port configuration and introduces a topology referred to as the Multi-Port Z-Network Converter (MPZNC) that use the proposed grid connected Photovoltaic (PV) system. A gridconnected inverter can integrate N number of inputs into a single DC bus as well it features the ability to bypass the input and output circuits, while employing the boost function in contrast to conventional converters. This design requires only fewer components to amalgamate various energy sources. As a result it exhibits superior circuit characteristics and achieves enhanced conversion efficiencies. When compared to the conventional Z-Source Converter (ZSC), it offers an improved input-output voltage transformation ratio and a broader voltage control range. This article provided various details about the circuit design, operating principles, control mechanisms and simulation results to substantiate its technical feasibility to explore the performance of the proposed MPZNCfed Single Phase Five Level (SPFL) inverter. In this context a miniature laboratory model was developed that rated at 1.5 kW, 230 V and 50 Hz. Various results are outlined in this paper through the set of Hardware - in the - Loop (HIL) by using OPAL-RT devices. Final these outcomes indicate that the proposed converter achieves an efficiency of approximately 93.1% and provides twice the boosting duration compared to the conventional ZSC converter. © 2026 IEEE.},
