	affiliations = {Avanthi Institute of Engineering and Technology, Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Andhra Pradesh, India; GITAM (Deemed to Be University), Department of Electrical Electronics and Communication Engineering, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; Visakha Institute of Engineering & Technology, Electrical and Electronics Engineering Department, Andhra Pradesh, Narava, India; Aditya University, Department of Electrical and Electronics Engineering, Andhra Pradesh, Surampalem, India; Graphic Era Deemed to Be University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Dehradun, India; New Prince Shri Bhavani College of Engineering and Technology, Department of IT, Tamil Nadu, Chennai, India; Vardhaman College of Engineering, Department of EEE, Telangana, Hyderabad, India; CPGC Pvt. Ltd and ERG Foundation, Anakapalli, Andhra Pradesh, Visakhapatnam, India},
	abstract = {Wind energy based electric power conversion models are popular in many places to produce electricity for various customers. Among many models, Permanent Magnet Synchronous Generator (PMSG) coupled wind energy conversion systems are attracting and utilized for many applications, especially under medium power ranges. In order to harvest maximum energy from wind turbine under every operating condition, a Maximum Power Point Tracking (MPPT) device must be used. An efficient MPPT approach, controller and a power converter will be parts in MPPT device. Usually many converters especially DC to DC converters are used as a MPPT converter. But unfortunately they are suffering from lack of high gain which is unable to produce sufficient output especially under low wind speeds. Hence, a switched-capacitor based high gain non-isolated DC to DC step up converter is implemented in this paper to utilized as a MPPT converter. Further, two end control methodology is developed to regulate both voltage and current to achieve a fast response during the harvesting maximum power. The Perturb and Observe (P&O) algorithm is incorporated to the proposed control methodology. The proposed model can work effectively during lower wind speeds also due to its ability of high step-up gain. Hardware-in-the-Loop (HIL) is executed through the utilization of two OPAL-RT modules to demonstrate multiple responses of the suggested model across different operating scenarios. © 2025 IEEE.},
